<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>These figures assume:</b> - Standard 3-tab or architectural asphalt shingles - One layer of old roofing removal - Basic roof pitch (4/12 to 6/12) - Midwest or Southeast U.S. pricing - All necessary permits and disposal</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">The per-square-foot cost typically ranges from <b>$4.50 to $7.50</b> for asphalt shingles when you include materials, labor, tear-off, and disposal.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Breaking Down the Costs: Where Your Money Actually Goes</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Understanding the components helps you spot inflated line items. Here's the typical breakdown:</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Materials (40-50% of total cost)</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Asphalt Shingles:</b> - 3-tab shingles: $90-$120 per square (100 sq ft) - Architectural shingles: $120-$200 per square - Premium/designer shingles: $250-$400 per square</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Other Required Materials:</b> - Underlayment (synthetic): $50-$80 per roll (400 sq ft coverage) - Ice and water shield: $60-$100 per roll (200 sq ft coverage) - Starter strips: $30-$45 per bundle - Ridge cap shingles: $35-$55 per bundle - Ventilation (ridge vent): $2-$4 per linear foot - Flashing (aluminum): $10-$25 per piece - Nails and fasteners: $100-$200 total</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Pro tip:</b> Material costs are fairly consistent. If a quote shows shingles at $400 per square when you can verify they cost $150, you've found padding.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Labor (40-50% of total cost)</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics, the median hourly wage for roofers in 2023 was <b>$23.51</b>. With employer costs (taxes, insurance, workers' comp), the real cost is approximately <b>$35-$45 per hour</b> per worker.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A typical 2,000 sq ft roof requires: - <b>Crew size:</b> 3-5 workers - <b>Time:</b> 2-3 days for removal and installation - <b>Total labor hours:</b> 48-72 hours - <b>Fair labor cost:</b> $4,500-$6,500</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If you're seeing labor charges above $8,000 for a straightforward 2,000 sq ft roof, question it.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Tear-Off and Disposal (10-15% of total cost)</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Removing old roofing and disposing of debris typically costs: - <b>Labor for removal:</b> $1-$1.50 per square foot - <b>Dumpster rental:</b> $400-$800 - <b>Disposal fees:</b> Varies by locality, $50-$200</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">For a 2,000 sq ft roof: <b>$2,400-$3,800 total</b></span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>Permits and Inspections (2-5% of total cost)</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Roofing permits typically cost $150-$500 depending on your municipality. This is non-negotiable and verifiable with a phone call to your local building department.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Material Costs: Beyond Asphalt Shingles</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If you're considering alternative materials, here are realistic price ranges:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Metal Roofing:</b> - Standing seam metal: $10-$16 per sq ft installed - Metal shingles: $8-$12 per sq ft installed - 2,000 sq ft roof: $16,000-$32,000</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Tile Roofing:</b> - Concrete tile: $10-$18 per sq ft installed - Clay tile: $15-$25 per sq ft installed - 2,000 sq ft roof: $20,000-$50,000</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Slate Roofing:</b> - Natural slate: $20-$35 per sq ft installed - 2,000 sq ft roof: $40,000-$70,000</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Synthetic/Composite:</b> - Rubber slate: $8-$12 per sq ft installed - Composite shingles: $7-$11 per sq ft installed - 2,000 sq ft roof: $14,000-$24,000</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>7 Factors That Legitimately Affect Price</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Some cost variations are justified. Here's what actually matters:</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>1. Roof Pitch (Slope)</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Steeper roofs are more dangerous and time-consuming. Pricing adjustments by pitch: - <b>4/12 to 6/12 (walkable):</b> Base price - <b>7/12 to 9/12:</b> Add 10-20% - <b>10/12 to 12/12:</b> Add 25-40% - <b>Above 12/12:</b> Add 50-75%, requires specialized equipment</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A 2,000 sq ft roof at 8/12 pitch might legitimately cost $13,500 instead of $12,000.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>2. Number of Layers</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Most building codes allow only two layers of shingles. Removing multiple layers costs more: - <b>One layer removal:</b> Standard price - <b>Two layers:</b> Add $1-$2 per sq ft ($2,000-$4,000 for 2,000 sq ft roof)</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>3. Roof Complexity</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Simple gable roofs cost less than complex designs with: - Multiple valleys (add $200-$400 each) - Dormers (add $150-$300 each) - Skylights (add $200-$500 each to reflash) - Chimneys (add $300-$600 for proper flashing) - Multiple facets and angles (increases waste and labor)</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>4. Regional Labor Rates</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Geography matters. According to BLS data, hourly roofing wages vary significantly:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Highest cost regions:</b> - Massachusetts: $31.40/hour median - Hawaii: $30.85/hour - New York: $29.15/hour - California: $28.20/hour</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Lowest cost regions:</b> - Arkansas: $17.50/hour median - Mississippi: $17.85/hour - Louisiana: $18.20/hour - Alabama: $18.65/hour</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Expect 20-40% higher costs in expensive markets.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>5. Deck Repair</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If your roof decking (plywood/OSB underneath) is damaged, it needs replacement: - <b>OSB sheathing:</b> $35-$50 per sheet (32 sq ft coverage) - <b>Labor to replace:</b> $50-$75 per sheet - <b>Typical damage:</b> 5-20% of roof area</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">For a 2,000 sq ft roof with 15% deck damage: add $1,200-$2,000.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">This is a legitimate add-on, but it should be identified during tear-off, not included upfront without inspection.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>6. Ventilation Requirements</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Proper attic ventilation prevents premature shingle failure. Requirements: - 1 sq ft of ventilation per 150 sq ft of attic space (building code) - Ridge vent: $2-$4 per linear foot - Soffit vents: $8-$15 each - Gable vents: $30-$60 each</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Budget $400-$1,200 for comprehensive ventilation upgrades.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>7. Time of Year</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Seasonal pricing is real: - <b>Peak season (summer):</b> 10-20% premium - <b>Off-season (late fall/winter):</b> 10-15% discount - <b>Emergency/storm damage:</b> 20-50% premium</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Red Flags in Roofing Quotes</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Here's what should make you pause—or walk away:</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #1: No Itemized Breakdown</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">A legitimate quote lists: - Material costs by type and quantity - Labor hours or days - Specific work to be performed - Tear-off and disposal costs - Permit fees</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> "Complete roof replacement: $18,500"</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> You can't verify if individual components are fairly priced. This is where overcharging hides.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #2: "Our Special Price Expires Today"</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">High-pressure tactics are a major warning sign. Roofing contractors with good reputations don't need to pressure you.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> "We're in your neighborhood today and can give you 40% off—but only if you sign now."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> This is classic storm-chasing behavior. The "discount" is often from an inflated price.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #3: Requires Full Payment Upfront</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Standard practice is: - 10-20% deposit to secure materials - 40-50% at project midpoint (tear-off complete) - Remaining 30-40% upon completion and approval</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> "We need $15,000 before we start work."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> You lose all leverage if problems arise. Some contractors take deposits and disappear.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #4: Material Costs 2-3x Retail Price</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">You can verify shingle prices yourself: - Check Home Depot/Lowe's online prices - Call local roofing supply stores - Check manufacturer websites</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> Quote shows $500/square for shingles you can buy for $180/square.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> A reasonable markup is 20-40%. Triple the cost is pure padding.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #5: Vague or Missing Insurance Information</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Legitimate contractors carry: - General liability insurance ($1-2 million minimum) - Workers' compensation insurance - License verification (in states that require it)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> "Oh, we're insured, don't worry about it."</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> If a worker is injured or your property is damaged, you could be liable. Always verify insurance independently by calling the carrier.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #6: Extreme Low Bids</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If one quote is 40-50% lower than others, investigate: - Are they licensed and insured? - Are they including all necessary work? - What's the payment structure? - Do they have verifiable references?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>What it looks like:</b> Three quotes at $12K-$14K, one at $7,500.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's a problem:</b> It could be a scam, or they're cutting corners (no underlayment, minimal flashing, poor nailing patterns). You'll pay more to fix it later.</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>🚩 Red Flag #7: Cash Discount</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Some contractors offer 10-15% off for cash payment.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Why it's suspicious:</b> They're likely avoiding taxes, which means they're probably not paying for proper insurance or workers' comp either. You're exposed to risk, and the "savings" aren't worth it.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>How to Negotiate (Without Being Unreasonable)</b></span></h2>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>When Negotiation Makes Sense</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Good reasons to negotiate:</b> - You have multiple comparable quotes and one is significantly higher - You're flexible on timing (off-season discount) - You've identified specific line items that seem inflated - You're willing to do some work yourself (cleanup, permit pickup)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Bad reasons to negotiate:</b> - "My neighbor paid less" (different roof, different scope) - "I saw online it should cost less" (without comparable data) - Trying to get premium materials at 3-tab prices</span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>What You Can Say</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Approach it professionally:</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><i>"I've gotten three quotes, and yours is about 20% higher. I prefer to work with you based on your reputation, but I need to understand the difference. Can we review the labor costs and material markups?"</i></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Focus on specific items:</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><i>"I see you've quoted $400 per square for architectural shingles. I've verified these retail for $165 per square. Can you explain your markup? I understand there's overhead, but I want to make sure I'm paying fairly."</i></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Ask for alternatives:</b></span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><i>"The total is beyond my budget. If we used standard 3-tab shingles instead of architectural, or if I'm flexible on timing, what could we do?"</i></span></p>
<h3 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.0px 0.0px; font: 14.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0d4e36; -webkit-text-stroke: #0d4e36"><span class="s1"><b>When to Walk Away</b></span></h3>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Walk away if:</b> - The contractor won't provide an itemized quote - They pressure you or use scare tactics - They can't provide proof of insurance when requested - Your gut says something is off - They demand unusual payment terms - References can't be verified or are suspicious</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Trust your instincts. A bad contractor will cost you far more than the difference between quotes.</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>The Regional Factor: What Location Does to Your Price</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">Using BLS data and cost-of-living adjustments, here's how region affects the same 2,000 sq ft asphalt shingle roof:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Northeast:</b> $13,000-$17,000 - Higher labor rates - Stricter building codes - More ice/water shield requirements</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Mid-Atlantic:</b> $11,500-$15,500 - Moderate labor rates - Standard material requirements</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Southeast:</b> $9,000-$13,000 - Lower labor costs - Hurricane-rated materials may add cost in coastal areas</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Midwest:</b> $9,500-$13,500 - Moderate costs overall - Winter weather may require upgraded underlayment</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Southwest:</b> $10,000-$14,000 - Moderate labor rates - Tile roofing more common (higher cost)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>West Coast:</b> $12,000-$18,000 - High labor costs (CA, WA especially) - Stricter environmental regulations - Fire-resistant materials may be required in some areas</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Mountain States:</b> $10,000-$14,500 - Moderate labor costs - Steeper pitches common (increases cost) - Snow load requirements</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>When Overcharging Becomes Fraud</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">There's a difference between high pricing and illegal behavior:</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Legal but expensive:</b> - Premium pricing (as long as work is performed as promised) - High profit margins - Expensive materials (if disclosed)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Illegal fraud:</b> - Charging for materials not used - Billing for work not performed - Misrepresenting material quality ("premium" shingles that are actually standard) - Abandoning jobs after receiving payment - Insurance fraud (inflating claims)</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">If you suspect fraud, document everything and report to: - Your state attorney general's office - State contractor licensing board - Better Business Bureau - Federal Trade Commission (ftc.gov/complaint)</span></p>
<h2 style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 14.9px 0.0px; font: 18.0px 'Helvetica Neue'; color: #0f6745; -webkit-text-stroke: #0f6745"><span class="s1"><b>Verify Your Quote the Smart Way</b></span></h2>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">You've got the data. Now what?</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">The roofing industry has a transparency problem, and most homeowners don't have time to become experts overnight. That's exactly why we built <b>Ungouge.ai</b>.</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1"><b>Here's how it works:</b> 1. Upload your roofing quote (any format: PDF, photo, email) 2. Our AI analyzes each line item against real market data 3. You get a clear breakdown of what's fair, what's high, and what's inflated 4. No email required, no "free quote" calls, no lead generation</span></p>
<p class="p2"><span class="s1">We're not here to sell you roofing services or connect you with contractors. We're here to give you the data to make an informed decision.</span></p>
